WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:05.220
Before Bill Gates and Steve Jobs, there was
Alan Turing who is widely considered to be

00:00:05.220 --> 00:00:07.720
the father of modern computers.

00:00:07.720 --> 00:00:12.520
His greatest contribution was cracking coded
German messages that helped the Allies win

00:00:12.520 --> 00:00:13.750
World War Two.

00:00:13.750 --> 00:00:18.000
Yet he would later be betrayed by the country
he protected.

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:23.730
Turing was born on June 23, 1912, in a nursing
home in Maida Vale, an affluent district in

00:00:23.730 --> 00:00:24.939
central London.

00:00:24.939 --> 00:00:28.579
His father Julius was a civil servant who
was posted to India.

00:00:28.579 --> 00:00:32.480
His mother  Ethel was the daughter of the
chief engineer of the Madras Railway.

00:00:32.480 --> 00:00:36.770
They wanted their children, Alan and his brother
John, raised in England, so they returned

00:00:36.770 --> 00:00:37.770
home.

00:00:37.770 --> 00:00:41.870
In primary school, the headmistress quickly
saw Turing’s potential, saying she has “had

00:00:41.870 --> 00:00:46.239
clever boys and hardworking boys, but Alan
is a genius.”

00:00:46.239 --> 00:00:50.149
At the age of 13, he was packed off to boarding
school.

00:00:50.149 --> 00:00:56.100
On his first day, had to ride his bike 60 miles (97 km)
to get to Sherborne School in southwest England

00:00:56.100 --> 00:00:58.219
because of a public transport strike.

00:00:58.219 --> 00:01:01.730
He demonstrated impressive athletic ability
for much of his life.

00:01:01.730 --> 00:01:06.820
His best marathon time was 2 hours and 46
minutes which doesn’t look great by today’s

00:01:06.820 --> 00:01:10.710
standards but at the time, it nearly qualified
him for the Olympics.

00:01:10.710 --> 00:01:13.640
An injury would put a stop to that dream.

00:01:13.640 --> 00:01:16.570
His calling would lie in mathematics.

00:01:16.570 --> 00:01:19.780
At school, some of his teachers were not impressed.

00:01:19.780 --> 00:01:25.220
On his report card for his second last year
at Sherborne, his English teacher noted: “Reading weak.

00:01:25.220 --> 00:01:28.619
Essays show ideas but are more grandiose than
pointed.”

00:01:28.619 --> 00:01:32.659
Despite the less-than-stellar performance
in some subjects, he showed brilliance in

00:01:32.659 --> 00:01:34.509
the ones he loved.

00:01:34.509 --> 00:01:39.810
Before leaving boarding school, he had grasped
the most complicated works of Newton and Einstein,

00:01:39.810 --> 00:01:43.899
forcing the headmaster to acknowledge that
his young scholar was: “A gifted &amp; distinguished

00:01:43.899 --> 00:01:47.909
boy, whose future career we shall watch with
much interest.”

00:01:47.909 --> 00:01:52.320
Turing shared his interest in math and science
with Christopher Morcom, a fellow student

00:01:52.320 --> 00:01:53.469
at Sherborne.

00:01:53.469 --> 00:01:58.430
A deep friendship developed; Morcom has been
described as Turing’s ‘first love.’

00:01:58.430 --> 00:02:00.340
Though the feeling was not reciprocated.

00:02:00.340 --> 00:02:06.180
Morcom died unexpectedly while in his teens
from bovine tuberculosis, which he had contracted

00:02:06.180 --> 00:02:08.090
from drinking tainted milk.

00:02:08.090 --> 00:02:10.450
Turing was overcome by sorrow.

00:02:10.450 --> 00:02:14.840
He was determined, however, to continue his
own journey to Cambridge University, writing

00:02:14.840 --> 00:02:19.200
to Morcom’s mother, “I know I must put
as much energy if not as much interest into

00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:23.709
my work, as if he were alive, because that
is what he would like me to do.”

00:02:23.709 --> 00:02:26.420
Turing was awarded first-class honors in mathematics.

00:02:26.420 --> 00:02:30.190
He was elected a Fellow of King’s College,
Cambridge soon after.

00:02:30.190 --> 00:02:33.739
And then he made the journey to the U.S. for a
Ph.D. at Princeton.

00:02:33.739 --> 00:02:39.470
In 1936, at the age of 23, he published his
epoch paper on the Turing Machine,

00:02:39.470 --> 00:02:44.090
a mathematical model that described
a device that could solve any problem, performing

00:02:44.090 --> 00:02:47.019
any task for which a program could be written.

00:02:47.019 --> 00:02:51.120
Turing had, in effect, conceived the world’s
first computer.

00:02:51.120 --> 00:02:54.700
While at Princeton, he went on to
build the first stages of an electro-mechanical

00:02:54.700 --> 00:03:00.569
Binary Multiplier - a machine for multiplying
binary numbers that may have sparked his interest

00:03:00.569 --> 00:03:01.950
in cryptology.

00:03:01.950 --> 00:03:06.920
At the time, he would have no idea how
important this would be in defeating the forces

00:03:06.920 --> 00:03:07.920
of evil.

00:03:07.920 --> 00:03:12.739
Hitler’s stormtroopers were settling into
the Rhineland - an area along the Rhine river

00:03:12.739 --> 00:03:17.690
in Western Germany that was supposed to remain
demilitarized according to the Treaty of Versailles

00:03:17.690 --> 00:03:19.360
that ended World War One.

00:03:19.360 --> 00:03:24.420
The reoccupation would be the beginning of
the path to all-out war three years later.

00:03:24.420 --> 00:03:28.610
In 1938, when he returned to his fellowship
at Cambridge University, he was recruited

00:03:28.610 --> 00:03:31.269
to join the Government Code and Cypher School
part-time.

00:03:31.269 --> 00:03:35.750
And when war broke out the following year,
Turing moved over full-time to the organization’s

00:03:35.750 --> 00:03:39.819
headquarters at Bletchley Park - a sprawling
estate north of London.

00:03:39.819 --> 00:03:44.569
It was here that he focused on cracking the
infamous Enigma code used by Nazi Germany’s

00:03:44.569 --> 00:03:47.129
military to send secret messages.

00:03:47.129 --> 00:03:50.590
Everything from weather reports to strategic
information.

00:03:50.590 --> 00:03:52.780
The Enigma looked like a typewriter.

00:03:52.780 --> 00:03:55.879
It substituted one letter for another to encrypt
a message.

00:03:55.879 --> 00:03:59.299
A letter coming into the rotor and coming
out would look different.

00:03:59.299 --> 00:04:05.769
The clever thing about it is that there were
103 sextillion possible combinations!

00:04:05.769 --> 00:04:10.261
Historian and wartime codebreaker Asa Briggs
wrote: “You needed exceptional talent, you

00:04:10.261 --> 00:04:14.860
needed genius at Bletchley, and Turing’s
was that genius.”

00:04:14.860 --> 00:04:18.320
Polish mathematicians had already figured
out how to read the messages before their

00:04:18.320 --> 00:04:22.720
country was invaded in 1939 and shared the
information with the Allies.

00:04:22.720 --> 00:04:27.790
But at the outbreak of war, the Germans began
to change the settings on the machine every

00:04:27.790 --> 00:04:33.000
midnight Berlin time which made it all but
impossible to decipher the thousands of messages

00:04:33.000 --> 00:04:34.820
sent every day.

00:04:34.820 --> 00:04:39.340
Turing and fellow codebreaker Gordon Welchman
developed a machine called “The Bombe”

00:04:39.340 --> 00:04:43.770
that was designed to discover which settings
Enigma used to scramble messages.

00:04:43.770 --> 00:04:48.120
It replicated the action of several Enigma
machines wired together.

00:04:48.120 --> 00:04:52.880
Each of the rapidly rotating drums simulated
the action of an Enigma rotor.

00:04:52.880 --> 00:04:55.390
The Bombe managed to crack Enigma.

00:04:55.390 --> 00:04:58.380
Bletchley Park became a code-breaking factory.

00:04:58.380 --> 00:05:03.010
200 Bombes cracked messages sometimes within
an hour or two of it being transmitted by

00:05:03.010 --> 00:05:04.010
the Nazis.

00:05:04.010 --> 00:05:05.840
Or even within minutes.

00:05:05.840 --> 00:05:12.550
As early as 1943, Turing’s machines cracked
84,000 messages a month or a staggering message

00:05:12.550 --> 00:05:14.140
every two minutes.

00:05:14.140 --> 00:05:18.010
He then turned his attention to the more complex
naval enigmas.

00:05:18.010 --> 00:05:20.050
This work was crucial.

00:05:20.050 --> 00:05:24.800
German U-Boats - submarines - were hunting
down so many Allied ships in the Atlantic

00:05:24.800 --> 00:05:29.800
that prime minister Winston Churchill feared
that the UK would starve of food, raw materials,

00:05:29.800 --> 00:05:31.470
and war supplies.

00:05:31.470 --> 00:05:36.630
Thankfully, Turing headed a team that managed
to decode the naval signals by 1941 so that

00:05:36.630 --> 00:05:41.020
Allied ships could better avoid these coordinated
submarine attacks.

00:05:41.020 --> 00:05:46.740
If Turing failed to crack the Enigma code,
the Allied invasion of Europe in 1944 - D-day -

00:05:46.740 --> 00:05:49.380
would have been delayed by at least a year.

00:05:49.380 --> 00:05:53.830
Hitler could have strengthened his coastal
defences by then to withstand the Allied assault.

00:05:53.830 --> 00:05:56.530
Yet there was still more work to be done.

00:05:56.530 --> 00:06:00.400
The Germans had an even more sophisticated
cipher machine.

00:06:00.400 --> 00:06:05.900
The Lorenz was personally used by Hitler and
his top generals to communicate the most high-level

00:06:05.900 --> 00:06:06.900
messages.

00:06:06.900 --> 00:06:08.440
Lorenz had more wheels than Enigma.

00:06:08.440 --> 00:06:10.870
The pattern was far more complex.

00:06:10.870 --> 00:06:14.910
Turing developed a code-breaking technique
called “Turingery” that fed into work

00:06:14.910 --> 00:06:19.570
by others including Captain Jerry Roberts
to crack the uncrackable Lorenz.

00:06:19.570 --> 00:06:23.680
And by the end of the war, Turing also
developed a device that allowed people to

00:06:23.680 --> 00:06:28.240
speak securely over the phone or the radio
by scrambling their voices.

00:06:28.240 --> 00:06:30.720
Delilah was years ahead of its time.

00:06:30.720 --> 00:06:35.290
It was portable unlike the secure phone system
connecting 10 Downing Street and the White

00:06:35.290 --> 00:06:40.610
House that was so large it had to be installed
in the basement of a department store on London’s

00:06:40.610 --> 00:06:42.140
Oxford street.

00:06:42.140 --> 00:06:46.210
Some historians estimate that the work at
Bletchley shortened the war by at least two

00:06:46.210 --> 00:06:52.250
years and saved at least 14 million lives
if not many millions more.

00:06:52.250 --> 00:06:56.660
And without it, the outcome of the war could
have been very different.

00:06:56.660 --> 00:06:59.690
One might expect the United Kingdom to have
been grateful.

00:06:59.690 --> 00:07:02.680
However, Turing’s work was top secret.

00:07:02.680 --> 00:07:06.950
He had to sign the Official Secrets Act which
prohibited him from disclosing anything about

00:07:06.950 --> 00:07:09.270
his time at Bletchley Park.

00:07:09.270 --> 00:07:12.970
Wartime information wouldn’t be declassified
until the seventies.

00:07:12.970 --> 00:07:17.200
So while Turing was awarded an Order of the
British Empire Medal at the end of the conflict,

00:07:17.200 --> 00:07:21.240
there was no public acknowledgment of his
contribution to the war effort.

00:07:21.240 --> 00:07:25.040
Many people learned of it for the first time
when the Oscar-winning film The Imitation

00:07:25.040 --> 00:07:27.850
Game was released in 2014.

00:07:27.850 --> 00:07:32.250
After the war, Turing took up a post at the
University of Manchester focused on creating

00:07:32.250 --> 00:07:34.850
the first functioning British computers.

00:07:34.850 --> 00:07:38.890
He was also among the first to conceive of
artificial intelligence.

00:07:38.890 --> 00:07:43.360
He had an idea that computers would become
so powerful that they would think.

00:07:43.360 --> 00:07:48.180
He devised the Turing Test, also called the
imitation game, as a way of determining whether

00:07:48.180 --> 00:07:50.180
a computer can think like a person.

00:07:50.180 --> 00:07:52.730
An observer questions a human and a computer.

00:07:52.730 --> 00:07:56.410
And has to decide which is the human and which
is the computer.

00:07:56.410 --> 00:08:01.380
If the computer can engage in a conversation
with a human without being detected as a machine,

00:08:01.380 --> 00:08:04.400
it is deemed to have demonstrated human intelligence.

00:08:04.400 --> 00:08:10.120
A computer program is said to have passed
the Turing test in 2014 by simulating a 13-year-old

00:08:10.120 --> 00:08:11.660
Ukrainian boy.

00:08:11.660 --> 00:08:17.321
In 1951, Turing received the UK's highest
scientific award when he was elected a fellow

00:08:17.321 --> 00:08:20.520
of the Royal Society, the national science
academy.

00:08:20.520 --> 00:08:23.100
However, his elation was short-lived.

00:08:23.100 --> 00:08:27.080
A year later, he was convicted of homosexual
acts.

00:08:27.080 --> 00:08:32.120
He had reported a burglary to police but changed
details of his story to cover up his relationship

00:08:32.120 --> 00:08:35.159
with a 19-year-old man who confessed to knowing
the culprit.

00:08:35.159 --> 00:08:40.330
He later admitted to the relationship and
was convicted of ‘gross indecency’.

00:08:40.330 --> 00:08:43.300
Homosexuality was a criminal offense at the
time.

00:08:43.300 --> 00:08:48.020
Turing didn’t want to go to jail so he agreed
to chemical castration - a hormone treatment

00:08:48.020 --> 00:08:51.040
that was supposed to suppress his sexual desires.

00:08:51.040 --> 00:08:55.830
A decade earlier, he withdrew his marriage
proposal to cryptanalyst Joan Clarke who also worked

00:08:55.830 --> 00:09:01.240
on Enigma after explaining his sexuality which
came as no surprise to her.

00:09:01.240 --> 00:09:05.500
Ironically, because he now had a criminal
record, he was prohibited from ever working

00:09:05.500 --> 00:09:08.750
for the British government’s postwar codebreaking
centre.

00:09:08.750 --> 00:09:12.030
He was shut out of the field that he helped
revolutionize.

00:09:12.030 --> 00:09:18.360
Two years later, in 1954, Turing was poisoned
by cyanide.

00:09:18.360 --> 00:09:20.839
He was 41 years old.

00:09:20.839 --> 00:09:24.450
A half-eaten Apple lay by his bedside when
he died.

00:09:24.450 --> 00:09:28.720
The speculation is that he bit into the apple
laced with the deadly chemical, however, the

00:09:28.720 --> 00:09:31.340
coroner never tested it for cyanide.

00:09:31.340 --> 00:09:33.870
The official verdict was suicide.

00:09:33.870 --> 00:09:39.210
It has been noted that the evidence is also
consistent with accidental poisoning.

00:09:39.210 --> 00:09:45.650
On September 10, 2009, Prime Minister Gordon
Brown publicly apologized for Turing’s treatment.

00:09:45.650 --> 00:09:50.110
He said, “…on behalf of the British government,
and all those who live freely thanks to Alan’s

00:09:50.110 --> 00:09:53.050
work, I am very proud to say: we’re sorry.

00:09:53.050 --> 00:09:54.550
You deserved so much better.”

00:09:54.550 --> 00:09:59.420
And in 2017, the UK Parliament passed the
“Alan Turing” law which wiped out the

00:09:59.420 --> 00:10:04.140
criminal records of 49,000 men convicted of
same-sex offences.

00:10:04.140 --> 00:10:08.590
Today, thanks to Alan Turing, we live in a
world where information - including this video

00:10:08.590 --> 00:10:11.610
- can be transmitted with just a click of a button.

00:10:11.610 --> 00:10:14.310
His work also made a myth about Apple’s
logo take hold.

00:10:14.310 --> 00:10:17.700
That it must surely pay
homage to Alan Turing.

00:10:17.700 --> 00:10:20.760
The man who designed the artwork, Rob Janoff,
denied it.

00:10:20.760 --> 00:10:26.350
And, before his own death in 2011, Steve Jobs
himself said: “It isn’t true, but God,

00:10:26.350 --> 00:10:28.320
we wish it were.”

00:10:28.320 --> 00:10:32.500
Turing helped kickstart the computer age that
has made it possible for you and me to access

00:10:32.500 --> 00:10:37.950
any information we like - including online
classes, which brings me to the sponsor of

00:10:37.950 --> 00:10:39.200
this video.

00:10:39.200 --> 00:10:42.310
Skillshare is like the Netflix of educational
videos.

00:10:42.310 --> 00:10:46.830
It’s an online learning community where
